<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isabella of Hainaut</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1170">1170</a>–<a href="/years/1190">1190</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isabella of Hainaut was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as the first wife of Philip II Augustus, whom she married in 1180. A daughter of Baldwin V of Hainaut and niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-count-of-flanders">Philip of Alsace</a>, count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, she brought Artois as her dowry, which passed to the French crown. She died in childbirth in 1190; her son became Louis VIII, and her descent linked the Capetians to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isabella-of-hainaut.jpg" alt="Isabella of Hainaut" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ealdgyth · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Isabella_of_Hainaut.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
